18,0.0029805839387966604,0.0009935279795988867,0.0003311759931996289,0.00011039199773320963,0.0003701306659110699,0.00079004355530369,0.0009300145184345633,0.0009766715061448545,0.0009883357530724272],"rank_high":[0.9483967935871744,0.6391115564462257,0.5597305722556223,0.7757552141319676,0.6670687053118582,0.5396575043090296,0.6575077452573185,0.5862367166889793,0.3520588654828327,0.2435720266037966,0.13023972458364794,0.06043025854489966,0.03481142084963456,0.04393714028321152,0.09064571342773718,0.4118819044759124,0.6929409522379562],"years":[2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024]}},"statement0":"<p style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\">The word <span  \"><i>\"spotlight\" </i></span> is mentioned two or more times in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0.1%</span> of psychology papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. </p>Among 2020-2024 papers containing <span  \"><i>\"spotlight\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">34.5%</span> of reported significant p-values (p < .05) are fragile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a far higher rate than the median Psychology paper (median shown for reference); relative to other words, papers with <span  \"><i>\"spotlight\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">99<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages (lower = better). Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability. Among papers where under 32% of significant p-values are fragile, the rate of successful replication was found to be 59.5%, whereas for papers over the replication rate was just 33.8% (see <a href=\"../assets/pdf/Bogdan_2025_PsyChange_Manuscript_SuppMat.pdf\">Supplemental Materials 7.3 of the mentioned paper</a>).","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span  \"><i>\"spotlight\" </i></span> tend to be published in higher impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">62<sup>nd</sup></span> percentile among all words)  but tend to be cited less (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">5<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are less likely to publish papers containing <span  \"><i>\"spotlight\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">43<sup>rd</sup></span> percentile). "}